{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CggZjSb1pvF3S57SBMctB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Model.** Tetrahedral d⁷ has a ⁴A₂ ground term, and its spin-allowed bands follow the same matrix as octahedral d³ with Dq taken as the tetrahedral value.\n\n- ν₁ = ⁴T₂(F) ← ⁴A₂ = 10Dq.\n- ν₂ and ν₃ are the two ⁴T₁ (F and P) levels, which mix through a 2×2 matrix:\n  - ν₂,₃ = (15B + 30Dq)/2 ∓ ½·√[(15B − 6Dq)² + 144B²]\n\n**Check ν₁ = 10Dq first.** This would give Dq = 433 cm⁻¹. The sum ν₂ + ν₃ = 15B + 30Dq = 26 160 then gives B ≈ 878 cm⁻¹. These values predict ν₂ ≈ 5 600 cm⁻¹, but the observed band is at 7 520 cm⁻¹. The three bands are therefore not consistent with a simple ν₁ = 10Dq assignment.\n\n**Fit to ν₂ and ν₃ instead.** These two bands depend on both Dq and B.\n\n- Sum: 15B + 30Dq = 26 160, so B = 1744 − 2Dq.\n- Difference: √[(15B − 6Dq)² + 144B²] = 11 120.\n- Substituting gives 1872Dq² − 2 888 064Dq + 998 672 384 = 0, whose physical root is Dq ≈ 523 cm⁻¹.\n- Then B ≈ 1744 − 1047 ≈ 697 cm⁻¹. This is about 72% of the free-ion value of roughly 970 cm⁻¹, which is a reasonable nephelauxetic reduction for Co(II).\n\n**Caveat.** With these values the predicted ν₁ = 10Dq ≈ 5 230 cm⁻¹, against 4 330 cm⁻¹ observed. That is a discrepancy of about 900 cm⁻¹. It is plausible in real Co(II) tetrahedral complexes, where spin–orbit coupling and mixing with the doublet states shift the bands. It also means the values are approximate. They come from the two upper bands only, and ν₁ is not reproduced.\n\n**Result:** Dq ≈ 520 cm⁻¹ (10Dq ≈ 5 200 cm⁻¹) and B ≈ 700 cm⁻¹.\n\n{\"Dq\": 523, \"B\": 697, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq and B were fitted to the ⁴T₁(F) and ⁴T₁(P) bands (ν₂, ν₃), and the predicted ν₁ = 10Dq of about 5230 cm⁻¹ misses the observed 4330 cm⁻¹ by about 900 cm⁻¹.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cach